### 3.5 Stampede control (stdlib only — no new module)

`go.mod` has no third-party modules, and the codex directs us to stay on the
existing in-process pieces, so stampede protection is implemented with the
standard library only — no `golang.org/x/...` import. A small in-flight map
lives in the decorator:

```go
type inflight struct{ once sync.Once; val []store.Order; err error }

func (o *orders) recentByUser(userID int64, limit int) ([]store.Order, error) {
    key := fmt.Sprintf("recent-orders:%d:%d", userID, limit)
    if v, ok := o.c.Get(key); ok {
        return v.([]store.Order), nil
    }
    o.mu.Lock()
    f, ok := o.inflight[key]
    if !ok {
        f = &inflight{}
        o.inflight[key] = f
    }
    o.mu.Unlock()

    f.once.Do(func() {
        f.val, f.err = o.inner.RecentByUser(userID, limit)
        if f.err == nil {
            o.c.SetTTL(key, f.val, o.ttl)
        }
        // drop the in-flight marker on a fresh critical-section
        o.mu.Lock()
        if o.inflight[key] == f { delete(o.inflight, key) }
        o.mu.Unlock()
    })
    return f.val, f.err
}
```

Only the first caller for a key does the DB work; concurrent callers block on
`f.once` and share the result — all synchronous, all in-process (principles 1
and 3). Without this, a cold hot key would fan out to N parallel DB queries —
the exact load pattern we are trying to relieve.
